description:
crate-jmap-sharing-server/src/principal.rs:209-222 and notification.rs:105-118

Both /set handlers validate the destroy array in two passes:

    if let Some(bad) = destroy_arr.iter().find(|v| !v.is_string()) {
        return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
            'destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {bad}'
        )));
    }
    for id_val in destroy_arr {
        let id_str = match id_val.as_str() {
            Some(s) => s.to_owned(),
            None => continue, // unreachable: validated above
        };
        ...
    }

The first pass guarantees that every element is a string Value; the second pass then has a 'this cannot happen' continue arm that the comment explicitly admits is unreachable. The type system cannot prove the second arm is unreachable because the destroy_arr value type is unchanged between the two passes — both passes see Value, both have to handle the None case.

Two more idiomatic shapes:

(a) Collect-then-iterate:

    let ids: Vec<&str> = match destroy_arr
        .iter()
        .map(|v| v.as_str().ok_or(v))
        .collect::<Result<Vec<_>, _>>()
    {
        Ok(ids) => ids,
        Err(bad) => {
            return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(format!(
                'destroy: every element must be a string Id; got {bad}'
            )));
        }
    };
    for id_str in ids { ... }

This collapses the two passes into one fallible collect. The second loop has no unreachable arm because ids has type Vec<&str>, not Vec<Value>.

(b) Single-pass with early return on the first non-string:

    for id_val in &destroy_arr {
        let id_str = match id_val.as_str() {
            Some(s) => s.to_owned(),
            None => return Err(JmapError::invalid_arguments(...)),
        };
        ...
    }

This trades the two-pass guarantee for atomicity (no partial application before the validation error). Either shape removes the 'unreachable: validated above' comment.

What goes wrong for callers when this stays as-is: nothing functional. The cost is reader cognitive load: 'why does this code defend against a case the comment says cannot happen?' is the question every careful reader asks. Defense-in-depth is sound engineering, but here the defense is enforced by another arm of the same function — the type system would be more convincing.

Sibling-scope: this is the exact shape filed by JMAP-3t94.11 from the policy angle (RFC 8620 §3.6.2 enforcement). The idiom angle is separate: even granted the policy, the implementation has a structural flaw.
